  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nSprawling and persistent high pressure across the Great Lakes \nand Quebec will dominate surface conditions through midweek\nacross the Ozarks, with ridging aloft gradually re-building over\nthe central Plains through the week. Several weak shortwaves \nwill push across the Northern Plains over the course of the week\nahead of a parent trough approaching the Pacific Northwest, but\nthe shortwave energy doesn't penetrate as far south as our\nnorthwest CWA until Friday, so conditions will remain dry \nthrough most if not all of the work week.\n\nPoPs begin to increase from northwest to southeast on Friday,\nwith the highest chances (15-30%) generally north of Highway 54.\nThese PoPs creep southeast through the weekend, with areawide\n20-40% PoPs by Sunday. Friday is where models begin to differ\nfrom each other, with uncertainty accumulating in the evolution\nof repeated shortwave attempts to flatten the ridge in the \nPlains and those waves' interactions with a blocked low that \ndevelops off the eastern seaboard by the end of the week.\n\nOverall message for the long term forecast is a dry and fairly\nstatus quo fall week, with seasonable and consistent temperatures\nin the 70s to low 80s during the day and in the 50s overnight\nall week.",
